---
import Article from "../../../layouts/Article.astro";
import KeyTakeaway from "../../../components/KeyTakeaway.astro";
import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import ComparisonTable from "../../../components/ComparisonTable.astro";
---

<Article
  title="How Long Does Gloomhaven Actually Take?"
  description="Setup, a scenario, teardown and the full campaign — honest numbers, not box-back numbers."
  game="gloomhaven"
  gameTitle="Gloomhaven"
  category="Guide"
  product="hexagon-token-trays"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    The box says 60 to 120 minutes. That's the scenario, and only the scenario.
    It doesn't include finding the map tiles, sorting the monster standees,
    reading the scenario text, levelling up, or putting it all away — and those
    are where evenings actually go.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Budget a full evening per scenario: roughly 15–30 minutes of setup, 60–120 minutes of play, and 15 minutes of packing up. The full Gloomhaven campaign runs well past a hundred hours." />

  <h2>Where an Evening Really Goes</h2>

  <ComparisonTable title="A realistic Gloomhaven session">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Phase</th>
          <th>Gloomhaven</th>
          <th>Jaws of the Lion</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Getting the box out and open</td>
          <td>5 min</td>
          <td>2 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Map tiles, monsters, tokens</td>
          <td>15–30 min</td>
          <td>Minimal — printed maps</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Character setup and card selection</td>
          <td>5–15 min</td>
          <td>5–10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Playing the scenario</td>
          <td>60–120 min</td>
          <td>30–90 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Levelling, shopping, events</td>
          <td>10–20 min</td>
          <td>5–15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Packing away</td>
          <td>10–20 min</td>
          <td>5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Total</strong></td>
          <td><strong>~2.5–3.5 hours</strong></td>
          <td><strong>~1.5–2 hours</strong></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ComparisonTable>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="This is why campaigns stall">
    Very few groups abandon Gloomhaven because they stopped enjoying it. They
    abandon it because a scenario needs a three-hour block, and three-hour blocks
    are hard to schedule repeatedly. Knowing that in advance is the difference
    between finishing and shelving.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>The Full Campaign</h2>

  <p>
    Gloomhaven has 95+ scenarios, though no campaign path visits all of them —
    the map branches, and choices close some routes off. A typical playthrough
    covers a large fraction of them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Realistically, that's well over a hundred hours of play, spread across a year
    or more of regular sessions for most groups. Jaws of the Lion's 25 scenarios
    are a far more achievable tens of hours, which is a genuine argument in its
    favour — see
    <a href="/games/gloomhaven/gloomhaven-vs-jaws-of-the-lion/">the comparison</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What Makes Sessions Longer</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Player count.</strong> Four players means four hands to plan and four turns per round. Two-player scenarios move dramatically faster.</li>
    <li><strong>Analysis paralysis.</strong> Choosing two cards from a hand of eleven, with initiative mattering, is exactly the kind of decision that eats five minutes if you let it.</li>
    <li><strong>Rules lookups.</strong> Common early on, and each one stops the table.</li>
    <li><strong>Setup from scratch every time.</strong> If the box goes away fully sorted each session, you pay the sorting cost twice.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to Get It Down</h2>

  <div class="my-6 space-y-3">
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">BIGGEST WIN</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Sort the box properly, once</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Monster standees, ability decks and tokens each in their own labelled home turns a 25-minute setup into a 10-minute one — every session, forever. It's covered in <a href="/games/gloomhaven/gloomhaven-setup-and-organisation/">taming the box</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">EASY WIN</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Set up while people arrive</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">One person reads the scenario and builds the map while the others sort characters. Setup is highly parallelisable and almost nobody does it that way.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">EASY WIN</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Use a companion app for the town phase</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Levelling, shopping and campaign bookkeeping are the least interesting minutes of the evening. Handing them to an app reclaims a solid chunk of every session.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">EASY WIN</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Leave it set up if you can</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">A spare table changes the game entirely. If a scenario can stay out between sessions, you can play in ninety-minute chunks instead of needing a whole evening.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="Play two scenarios in a sitting">
    Once the box is out and everything is sorted, the second scenario of an
    evening costs far less setup than the first. Groups that play back-to-back get
    through the campaign roughly twice as fast in calendar terms.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Should the Time Commitment Put You Off?</h2>

  <p>
    Only if your group is unstable. Gloomhaven rewards consistency — the same four
    people, meeting regularly, developing characters over months. If that
    describes your group, the hundred hours are the point rather than the cost.
  </p>

  <p>
    If it doesn't, start with Jaws of the Lion. A 25-scenario campaign you finish
    is worth more than a 95-scenario campaign that stops at scenario nine.
  </p>
</Article>
